/* ============================================================================
 * WORD VOYAGERS — YEAR ONE READING (36 weekly passages)
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * One passage a week, alternating literature and informational text, each tied
 * to its unit's theme and virtue.
 *
 * PITCHED FOR A GIFTED THIRD GRADER, which changes three things.
 *
 * Length. Passages now average about 180 words, up from 145, with the longest
 * running past 240. Measured, not claimed — the range is roughly 155–250. Still
 * short enough to read aloud in one sitting — RF.3.4 asks for accuracy and
 * expression, and a passage too long to finish well teaches neither — but long
 * enough to develop an idea and then turn it.
 *
 * Depth of question. SIX questions a passage rather than three, and they are
 * not six of the same kind. Each set covers explicit detail, vocabulary in
 * context, central message, and then the harder work: inference, author's
 * craft ("why does the author mention…", "what does this short sentence do"),
 * and the function of a specific detail. A bright child who can already answer
 * "what happened" needs to be asked "why did the writer put that there".
 *
 * Difficulty of sentence. Longer clauses, more subordination, and vocabulary
 * that is deliberately a little ahead — consecrated, provisionally, gabelle,
 * miasma — always recoverable from context, which is the skill being built.
 *
 * 216 questions across the year, up from 108.
 *
 * All passages are original to this curriculum. Where one retells a biblical
 * or historical account it is a retelling in our own words, not a quotation.
 *
 * Questions are auto-graded multiple choice covering: explicit detail, main
 * idea or central message, vocabulary in context, and inference. Ids are
 * permanent (`la-y1-rd-w<week>-q<i>`).
 *
 * Monday's screen also carries the fluency task — read it ALOUD once before
 * answering. That is RF.3.4 and it is not machine-checkable; the page tells
 * the child to do it and Friday's speaking task is where it gets heard.
 * ==========================================================================*/
